grMatchingField -- Construct a matching field for the Grassmannian variety
**************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        L = grMatchingField(weightMatrix)
        L = grMatchingField(k, n, tuples)
  * Inputs:
      * k, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, positive integer; the size of
        the tuples of the matching field
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, positive integer; the tuples
        have entries in 1 .. n
      * weightMatrix, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, induces the
        matching field
  * Outputs:
      * L, an instance of the type *note GrMatchingField: GrMatchingField,, 

Description
===========

This function is the basic constructor for Grassmannian matching fields. The
function outputs an instance of type *note GrMatchingField: GrMatchingField,,
which represents the matching field and stores all data related and computed
about it.

There are two basic ways to define a Grassmannian matching field. The first way
is to supply a weight matrix that induces the matching field. This produces a
coherent matching field and is well-defined if the matrix is generic.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : M = matrix {{0,0,0,0,0,0}, {1,6,2,5,3,4}, {60,50,10,20,40,30}}          |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = | 0  0  0  0  0  0  |                                                   |
|     | 1  6  2  5  3  4  |                                                   |
|     | 60 50 10 20 40 30 |                                                   |
|                                                                             |
|              3       6                                                      |
|o1 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : L1 = grMatchingField M                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = Grassmannian Matching Field for Gr(3, 6)                                |
|                                                                             |
|o2 : GrMatchingField                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : getTuples L1                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|o3 = {{2, 1, 3}, {2, 1, 4}, {4, 1, 3}, {2, 4, 3}, {2, 1, 5}, {5, 1, 3}, {2,  |
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     5, 3}, {5, 1, 4}, {2, 5, 4}, {4, 5, 3}, {2, 1, 6}, {6, 1, 3}, {2, 6, 3},|
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     {6, 1, 4}, {2, 6, 4}, {4, 6, 3}, {5, 1, 6}, {2, 5, 6}, {6, 5, 3}, {6, 5,|
|     ------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|     4}}                                                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : List                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : isToricDegeneration L1                                                  |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = true                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

In the above example, we construct the Grassmannian matching field induced by
the given weight matrix. The tuples for the matching field are listed in RevLex
order. The function *note isToricDegeneration: isToricDegeneration, checks the
equality of the *note matchingFieldIdeal: matchingFieldIdeal, and the initial
ideal of the *note plueckerIdeal: plueckerIdeal, with respect to the weight
inducing the matching field.

The second way to define a Grassmannian matching field is to list out its
tuples.

+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : T = {{1,4}, {2,4}, {3,4}, {3,1}, {3,2}, {1,2}}  |
|                                                     |
|o5 = {{1, 4}, {2, 4}, {3, 4}, {3, 1}, {3, 2}, {1, 2}}|
|                                                     |
|o5 : List                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : L2 = grMatchingField(2, 4, T)                   |
|                                                     |
|o6 = Grassmannian Matching Field for Gr(2, 4)        |
|                                                     |
|o6 : GrMatchingField                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : getTuples L2                                    |
|                                                     |
|o7 = {{1, 2}, {3, 1}, {3, 2}, {1, 4}, {2, 4}, {3, 4}}|
|                                                     |
|o7 : List                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : isCoherent L2                                   |
|                                                     |
|o8 = true                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : getWeightMatrix L2                              |
|                                                     |
|o9 = | 0 0  0 0  |                                   |
|     | 0 -1 1 -2 |                                   |
|                                                     |
|              2       4                              |
|o9 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                               |
+-----------------------------------------------------+

As shown in the example above, the first argument "k" specifies the size of the
tuples. The third argument is a list of the tuples. Note that the tuples can be
supplied in any order. If the list of tuples is not correct, i.e. if some are
missing or duplicated then the function raises an error. When a Grassmannian
matching field is constructed in this way, it is not guaranteed to be coherent,
i.e., it may not be induced by a weight matrix. The function *note isCoherent:
isCoherent, checks whether the matching field is coherent and the function
*note getWeightMatrix: getWeightMatrix, returns a weight matrix that induces
the matching field, if it exists. If the matching field is not coherent, then
these methods produce an error.

A note of caution. Two different weight matrices may induce the same matching
field so the function *note getWeightMatrix: getWeightMatrix, may return a
weight matrix that is different to what may be expected. However, if a matching
field is defined by a weight matrix, then that weight matrix will be returned.

See also
========

  * *note GrMatchingField: GrMatchingField, -- the class of Grassmannian
    matching fields
  * *note FlMatchingField: FlMatchingField, -- The class of Matching Fields for
    partial flag varieties
  * *note flMatchingField: flMatchingField, -- Construct a matching field for a
    partial flag variety
  * *note isToricDegeneration: isToricDegeneration, -- Does the matching field
    give rise to a toric degeneration
  * *note plueckerIdeal: plueckerIdeal, -- The Pluecker ideal of a matching
    field
  * *note matchingFieldIdeal: matchingFieldIdeal, -- The toric ideal of a
    matching field
  * *note isCoherent: isCoherent, -- Is the matching field coherent
  * *note getWeightMatrix: getWeightMatrix, -- weight matrix that induces the
    matching field
